**Register Shifts and Code-Switching**

**Shifting Formality:** While the author’s default style is very informal and slang-rich, there are notable moments where they **code-switch to a much more formal register**. These usually occur when context demands it – for example, when communicating with someone older or in a position of authority, or when conveying gravely serious content. A stark illustration is when the author drafts a letter to his girlfriend’s parents asking permission to date her. In the text thread, his style abruptly changes from casual to **polite and formal English**: *“I'm reaching out to you because I would like to ask for your permission to see your daughter, Anne. I hope you will allow me to address some of the obvious concerns...”*. In this message, he uses no slang, fully written words (*“I would like to ask”* instead of “wanna ask”), proper punctuation, and even the respectful term “your daughter, Anne” instead of just Anne. The tone is measured and almost bureaucratic, which is a **dramatic departure** from his usual emotive candor. It reads like a carefully crafted email or letter, indicating the author is capable of suppressing his personal voice in favor of a more standardized English when necessary.

After drafting that formal message (likely to be sent or shown externally), he immediately reverts to his normal tone when back to talking to his girlfriend or friends. This suggests that the formal register is not his comfortable mode but rather a *conscious adaptation*. Another example of such adaptation is when coordinating logistics or dealing with third parties: in a 2025 exchange involving a friend and the girlfriend’s parents, the author instructs his friend with uncharacteristic corporate-sounding language: *“If you happen to talk to Ellen, please be mindful of my optics in what you say. [Name] doesn’t seem to realize how much her own inaction and denial has reflected poorly on me and I’m going to need to do some damage control.”*. Words like “mindful of my optics,” “reflected poorly,” “damage control” are **formal, PR-type phrasing** not seen in his casual interactions. He’s effectively code-switching to a strategic, professional tone because the situation (managing impressions with parents/Ellen) calls for that mindset. It stands out because normally he’d say something like “please don’t make me look bad, I have to fix this mess” – but instead he says “noticeably more productive” and “optics,” showing a deliberate shift in diction likely influenced by professional or strategic considerations.

