**Threading and Callbacks:** In multi-message bursts, the author might reference an earlier message in the thread implicitly, relying on context rather than explicitly quoting. For example, he might say **“that’s what I was afraid of”** in one message referring to something the partner said a few messages ago, without restating the whole idea (he trusts the partner to follow the thread). This reflects a **high-context conversational style** typical in texting threads – they both keep track of the conversation, so shorthand references suffice. He also uses pronouns like “that” or “it” across messages to tie ideas together (e.g., **“it didn't have to be like this. But this is where we are”** – “it” referring to the situation described in previous texts).

